All results describe the production code after the fixes merged into `topdisc`
(§4, §5.5). The first version of this study (old code) is on branch
`registrar-overhead-oct27`; §6.8 compares the two.

* **The registrar core is cheap per request; validation dominates.** A `REGTOPIC`
  that receives a wait ticket costs {{fit.RegisterFresh.T.small.intercept|f:0}} ns plus
  {{fit.RegisterFresh.T.small.slope|f:2}} ns per cached ad of the requested topic, and does not
  grow with occupancy (§6.1).{{if:share.cgo.core_fraction}} With record decoding, signature verification and
  ticket handling included, a full `REGTOPIC` costs about {{share.cgo.total_ns|sig:2}} ns, of which
  the registrar core is {{share.cgo.core_fraction|pct:1}}.{{endif}} A `TOPICQUERY` lookup visits every cached
  ad of the topic ({{fit.RandomNodes.T.all.slope|f:1}} ns each), regardless of the 16-result
  limit. All registrar work runs on the single Discovery v5 dispatch goroutine (§3.3).
* **With production advertisers, occupancy settles well below C.** At C = 1000 the
  time-averaged occupancy is {{demand.C1000-fresh0.25.utilisation|pct:1}} of C at
  {{demand.C1000-fresh0.25.fresh_attempt_rps|f:2}} fresh attempts/s and
  {{demand.C1000-fresh100.utilisation|pct:1}} at {{demand.C1000-fresh100.fresh_attempt_rps|f:0}}/s. Admissions saturate
  at {{demand.C1000-fresh100.admission_rps|f:2}}/s; further demand becomes abandoned attempts
  ({{demand.C1000-fresh100.abandon_fraction|pct:1}} of fresh attempts) and
  {{demand.C1000-fresh100.requests_per_admission|f:0}} `REGTOPIC` per admission. Admitted advertisers
  waited a median of {{demand.C1000-fresh5.cumwait_waited_median_s|f:0}} s (p95
  {{demand.C1000-fresh5.cumwait_waited_p95_s|f:0}} s; E = 900 s) at 5 attempts/s (§6.2).
* **Memory is dominated by the decoded ENR.** Each cached ad costs
  {{mem.C1000-empirical.independent.bytes_per_ad|ci:0}} B with typical records and
  {{mem.C1000-empirical-maxenr.independent.bytes_per_ad|f:0}} B with maximum-size records; a full
  default cache (C = 1000) of maximum-size records holds
  {{mem.C1000-empirical-maxenr.independent.at_capacity_mib|f:2}} MiB (§6.3). Lower-bound state stays
  bounded by the cache contents (§6.6).
* **Throughput.** {{if:rt.knee_requests_rps}}On one core the harness served up to {{rt.knee_requests_rps|sig:3}} requests/s
  without queueing (§6.4.2); the registrar core used at most {{rt.max_busy|pct:1}} of that core
  in the sweep.{{else}}*Provisional:* the realtime block produced no knee. From per-call costs, one core could
  serve roughly {{cap.implied_min|sig:2}}–{{cap.implied_max|sig:2}} registrar-core requests/s.{{endif}}
  {{if:eval10k.hot_5_busy}}The hottest registrar of the 10k-node runs would keep a core
  {{eval10k.hot_5_busy|pct:2}} busy (§6.7).{{endif}}
* **Findings (§4).** F1 (waiting-time overflow), F2 (IP-tree counters under churn)
  and F4 (ticket binding) are fixed, each with a regression test; G is restored and the
  default capacity is now 1000. F3 (ticket keys expire before a 24 h quote ends) is
  still open, as are the spec decisions D1, D2, D4 and D7 (§4.2).
